researchers have set up a system that  lets them see mutations in living cells  and follow the cell's fates good bad or  neutral spontaneous mutations are not  easy to detect in a living system but  there are important unanswered questions  about how mutations are distributed and  how common they are this information  makes it possible to use the mutation  rate of organisms to look back in time  to see when two related species diverged  in this field  the mother cells are along the top of a  microfluidic chip so named because  narrow channels control the liquids flow  in very specific ways the offspring but  off and expand into the tiny tubes each  cell pinched off from its progenitor  newly made cells are whisked away by  liquid at the bottom of the tube  bacteria are red and replication error  machinery markers from mutation are the  yellow dots the researchers followed  bacterial cells over 200 generations to  look for mutations and the effects those  mutations had they were able to  calculate the rate of deadly mutations  at about 1% much lower than expected  they also found that mutations occur  regularly in individual cells they all  get mutations at pretty much the same  rate regardless of stress level and that  mutations are less detrimental to the  bacteria and than expected this new  technique for tracking mutations and  single cells could be used in cancer  research or to explore different  mutation patterns in different tissues  [Music]
 

